<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolsena</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Volsinii Novi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolsena is a town on the north-eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-bolsena">Lake Bolsena</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It grew from Volsinii Novi, the new town where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> resettled the people of Etruscan Volsinii after destroying their city in 264 BCE. A Eucharistic miracle reported in its church of Santa Cristina in 1263 was linked to the institution of the feast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> and inspired Orvieto Cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bolsena.jpg" alt="Bolsena" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bolsena.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
